Description: Accept names with any non-ASCII character, as PHP Twig does
 Twig's REGEX_NAME is "[a-zA-Z_\x7f-\xff][a-zA-Z0-9_\x7f-\xff]*" without the
 /u flag, so PHP matches it byte by byte: every byte of a multi-byte UTF-8
 character is in \x7f-\xff, and names such as "名前" or "données" are valid.
 In JavaScript the same range means code points U+007F to U+00FF, so
 twig-lexer 1.0.0 throws "Unexpected character" on "名". Widening the range
 to \x7f-￿ reproduces PHP's behaviour for every code point (astral
 characters match through their surrogate halves). Applies to both levels.
Author: Numiko
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: not-needed
Twig-Reference: none (port fix; REGEX_NAME unchanged since Twig 2.x)
Test: test/fixtures/twig/names.twig (parity with Twig 3.28.0); npm run test:upstream
Last-Update: 2026-09-23
---
diff --git a/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts b/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
index 4b61057..4c088d2 100644
--- a/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
+++ b/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ export const lineSeparators: string[] = ['\\r\\n', '\\r', '\\n'];
 /**
  * The regular expression pattern used to identify a name.
  */
-export const namePattern: string = '[a-zA-Z_\\x7f-\\xff][a-zA-Z0-9_\\x7f-\\xff]*';
+export const namePattern: string = '[a-zA-Z_\\x7f-\\uffff][a-zA-Z0-9_\\x7f-\\uffff]*';
 
 /**
  * The regular expression pattern used to identify a number.
